EHAF v4.0: Consistency-Constrained Adaptation, Multi-Indicator Agency, and Agency-Preserving Safety for Extended Human Embodiment

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Version 4.0

Abstract

Extended human agency—the capacity to act through artificial bodies while retaining authorship over action and outcome—requires a theoretical foundation that is precise enough to be falsified. This dissertation develops the Extended Human Agency Framework (EHAF), a mathematical and architectural account of predictive embodiment, shared control, and cross-embodiment transfer. EHAF is organized around a single confirmatory core: the hypothesis that error consistency, not error magnitude or information-theoretic modelability, governs the asymptote of implicit sensorimotor adaptation, and that this effect is mediated by trial-to-trial modulation of error sensitivity b_t. This hypothesis is stated against an explicit rival—the non-linear motor-correction (NLMC) account—and is designed to survive or fail under matched error-size distributions. Three auxiliary structures complete the framework: (i) a three-factor body-model integration profile (E_{\text{own}}, E_{\text{sm}}, E_{\text{loc}}), with a supernumerary-limb dissociation test; (ii) a three-indicator agency measurement model (A_{\text{SA}}, A_{\text{IB}}, A_{\text{exp}}) in which the one-factor structure is expected to be rejected; and (iii) a delay-compensated, minimal-intervention safety layer built on a state–action control barrier function (Q-CBF), with sense of agency as a co-primary endpoint rather than a constraint. Cross-embodiment transfer is expressed in transition-kernel space for robot policies and in the COIN language of contextual inference for human learners. Every formal object carries a derivation, every proposition a refutation condition, and every instrument a validity argument. The framework is narrower than its predecessors, but it is finally adjudicable.

Keywords: extended human agency; sensorimotor adaptation; memory of errors; body ownership; sense of agency; shared control; control barrier functions; cross-embodiment transfer.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The problem of extended agency

Human action is increasingly mediated by artificial bodies: robotic avatars, supernumerary limbs, exoskeletons, and shared-autonomy systems in which the boundary between human intention and machine assistance is continuously renegotiated. The scientific question is not whether such systems can be built—they can—but what theoretical vocabulary is adequate to describe what happens to the human agent when action is extended through them. Three properties of this situation resist assimilation to standard human–computer interaction frameworks.

First, the relevant sensorimotor property of an artificial body is not the magnitude of the error it introduces but the structure of that error over time. A constant 100 ms latency and a variable 50 \pm 30 ms latency have the same mean, yet they may recruit different adaptation mechanisms, produce different asymptotic performance, and leave different residual deficits. A framework that treats latency as a scalar magnitude cannot capture this.

Second, embodiment is not unitary. The feeling that an artificial limb belongs to the self, the functional integration of that limb into the sensorimotor repertoire, and the perceived location of the self are dissociable. Treating them as measurements of a single latent variable is a measurement error with theoretical consequences.

Third, agency is not a single instrument. Sensory attenuation, intentional binding, and explicit judgments of control are routinely treated as interchangeable proxies for the sense of agency, yet they dissociate from one another. Intentional binding, in particular, appears to track sensorimotor predictability rather than authorship, and its relationship to explicit agency judgments collapses in human–computer dyads even when it holds in human–human dyads. A framework that assumes a monolithic agency construct will misattribute predictability effects to authorship.

EHAF v4.0 is a response to these three failures. It replaces magnitude with consistency, replaces a unitary embodiment construct with a tested multi-factor profile, and replaces a monolithic agency variable with a three-indicator measurement model whose one-factor structure is expected to be rejected.

1.2 The empirical pivot: consistency, not modelability

The predecessor of this framework (v1.0–v2.0) proposed that the relevant property of a sensorimotor error process is its modelability,

\mathcal{M}(e) = \frac{\mathcal{I}(e_{t-\tau:t-1}; e_t)}{\mathcal{H}(e_t)},

the fraction of the error’s entropy that is predictable from its own past. This quantity has two defects. It is not bounded in [0,1], because differential entropy can be negative. More importantly, it is not the variable that governs adaptation. The empirical record shows that when perturbations are variable, total implicit adaptation decreases because the implicit learning system modulates its error sensitivity in response to the consistency of past errors, not because it forgets more or because it fails to exploit autocorrelation. The implicit system becomes more sensitive to errors that occur in a consistent direction and less sensitive when errors are dispersed.

EHAF v4.0 therefore makes error consistency the primary theoretical object. Consistency is defined as

\kappa(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{e \sim P_\theta}\left[ \exp\left( -\frac{\|e - \bar{e}\|^2}{2\sigma_c^2} \right) \right],

a bounded functional of the error distribution that decreases monotonically with the eigenvalues of the error covariance. The adaptation model becomes

x_{t+1} = a x_t + b_t e_t, \qquad e_t = r_t - x_t + \varepsilon_t, \qquad b_t = b_0 \, \kappa(\theta_t),

so that error sensitivity is a state variable driven by the history of error consistency, not a fixed parameter. This is a conservative modification: the state-space form is standard, and the only change is that b is indexed by time and driven by a measurable property of the error distribution.

1.3 The rival that must be ruled out

A framework that makes consistency central must confront the non-linear motor-correction (NLMC) account. The implicit system exhibits a static non-linear correction function: correction rises with error size up to a saturation point and then declines for very large errors. When perturbation variance increases, the error distribution shifts into the declining region of this function, and the average correction is attenuated without any history-dependent change in sensitivity. On this account, high-variance environments reduce adaptation because they sample more large errors, not because they reduce consistency.

EHAF v4.0 does not assume that consistency wins. It is designed so that the NLMC alternative can win. The confirmatory core is a matched-error-size design in which the marginal error-size distribution is held fixed while temporal consistency is manipulated. Under memory-of-errors, higher consistency should raise b_t and lower asymptotic residual error. Under NLMC, with the error-size distribution fixed, there should be no effect of consistency. The framework's central proposition is a mediation hypothesis:

b_t = b_0 + \beta_1 \kappa(\theta_t) + \boldsymbol{\beta}_2^\top \mathbf{z}_t + \eta_t,
\qquad
e^\star = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \kappa(\theta) + \gamma_2 \bar{b} + \boldsymbol{\gamma}_3^\top \mathbf{z} + \zeta,

where \mathbf{z} includes mean error magnitude and the NLMC-predicted correction. The memory-of-errors pathway is the indirect effect \beta_1 \gamma_2; the direct effect \gamma_1 captures any residual consistency effect not mediated by sensitivity. The framework is refuted if \gamma_1 is null after residualizing on \mathbf{z}, or if the indirect effect is not positive and significant.

1.4 Three auxiliary structures

The adaptation core is the framework's confirmatory center, but extended agency is not only a sensorimotor phenomenon. Three auxiliary structures connect the core to ownership, agency, and safety.

Body-model integration profile. The Virtual Embodiment Questionnaire has a validated three-factor structure—ownership, agency, and perceived body-schema change. EHAF adds self-location as a fourth observable and tests whether the profile is better modeled as one, two, or three factors. The dissociation test uses an independent supernumerary robotic finger, which can be embodied while being more difficult to own than a substitute limb, and which engages body image while the body schema processes only one limb. Passive congruent visuo-tactile stimulation should raise ownership without changing sensorimotor integration; active motor training should raise sensorimotor integration without necessarily raising ownership.

Agency as a three-indicator model. Sensory attenuation, intentional binding, and explicit judgments of control are modeled as indicators of a latent agency factor. Confirmatory use of the latent variable requires that the one-factor model not be rejected. Given the documented dissociations—binding tracks predictability rather than authorship in human–computer dyads, and sensory attenuation dissociates from explicit judgments—the framework expects rejection and treats the divergence pattern as a finding. The confirmatory value lies not in confirming a latent factor but in formally establishing the structure that monolithic accounts assume away.

Agency-preserving safety. The safety layer is not a penalty term but a state–action control barrier function (Q-CBF) constraint that intervenes minimally and smoothly, avoiding the abrupt last-moment corrections of conventional filters. The human-centered safety filter (HCSF) of Oh et al. (2025) demonstrates that Q-CBF interventions can improve safety without compromising agency, and can boost agency relative to a conventional filter at matched safety. EHAF makes agency a co-primary endpoint and predicts a non-monotonic agency–authority curve: agency rises with assistance when assistance is aligned with intent, then falls when assistance overrides it. The safety layer is delay-compensated because latency is the framework's central experimental manipulation, and delay-free CBFs become unsafe once input delay is introduced.

1.5 Cross-embodiment transfer: two languages, not one

Human transfer and robot transfer are not the same learning problem. PAC-Bayes bounds describe a learner that selects a hypothesis from a class; human sensorimotor adaptation is better described as contextual inference over latent contexts, in which memory creation, updating, and expression are controlled by a single computation. EHAF therefore uses COIN for human transfer, with savings and spontaneous recovery as primary metrics, and confines PAC-Bayes to robot policy learning, where it is diagnostic rather than predictive. The robot transfer bound is expressed in transition-kernel space,

\operatorname{dis}_{\text{emb}}(\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{T}) = \sup_{x,u} \left\| P_{\mathcal{S}}(\cdot \mid x,u) - P_{\mathcal{T}}(\cdot \mid x,u) \right\|_{\text{TV}},

not marginal input discrepancy, because two embodiments can share state marginals while differing in action-conditioned dynamics. The claim that morphological similarity is non-monotonic in transfer is exploratory, not derived.

1.6 Contribution and structure

EHAF v4.0 makes four contributions. First, it replaces the unbounded and empirically unsupported modelability construct with a bounded consistency functional and a pre-registered mediation test against the NLMC rival. Second, it replaces the unitary embodiment construct with a tested multi-factor profile and a supernumerary-limb dissociation design. Third, it replaces the monolithic agency variable with a three-indicator model whose rejection is expected and informative. Fourth, it replaces the myopic one-step CBF with a delay-compensated, minimal-intervention Q-CBF and elevates agency to a co-primary endpoint of shared-control evaluation.

The remainder of the dissertation is organized as follows. Section 2 develops the corrected sensorimotor dynamics and the consistency–sensitivity mediation model, including the matched-error-size design and the NLMC control arm. Section 3 presents the body-model integration profile and the supernumerary dissociation. Section 4 develops the three-indicator agency model and the delay-compensated Q-CBF authority allocation. Section 5 addresses cross-embodiment transfer in both COIN and transition-kernel terms. Section 6 specifies the layered architecture and the interface contracts between its five layers. Section 7 states the falsifiable predictions and their refutation conditions. Section 8 is a pre-registered phenomenological audit that documents, but does not measure, the experiential boundary conditions of the quantitative variables. The appendices contain full derivations, identification proofs, and decision rules.

The framework is deliberately narrow. It does not measure subjective continuity; it does not claim that consistency is the only determinant of adaptation; it does not assume that embodiment is unitary, that agency is monolithic, or that similarity is monotonic in transfer. Its virtue is that each of these refusals is paired with a formal object that can be tested, and each test has a named alternative that can defeat it.


