1.2 Agency as a three-indicator measurement model

Definition 1 (Agency measurement model).

\mathbf{A} = \boldsymbol{\lambda} A + \boldsymbol{\varepsilon}, \qquad \boldsymbol{\varepsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\Theta}),

where \mathbf{A} = (A_{\text{SA}}, A_{\text{IB}}, A_{\text{exp}})^\top are sensory attenuation, intentional binding, and explicit agency judgment. Confirmatory use of A as a scalar requires that the one-factor model not be rejected at RMSEA < .08 and CFI > .95. If the model is rejected—as the dissociations in the literature predict—the three indicators are analyzed separately and their divergence is reported as a finding. Intentional binding in human–computer dyads tracks sensorimotor predictability rather than authorship, so A_{\text{IB}} is not a valid proxy for agency over a robot in the absence of a separate predictability control.

