3. Body-Model Profile: Three Factors and the Supernumerary Dissociation

3.1 The VEQ three-factor structure

The Virtual Embodiment Questionnaire (VEQ) has a confirmed three-factor structure: (1) ownership of a virtual body, (2) agency over a virtual body, and (3) perceived change in the body schema. Self-location is added as a separate item because it is dissociable from ownership.

Definition 3 (Body-model profile).

E = (E_{\text{own}}, E_{\text{sm}}, E_{\text{loc}}) \in [0,1] \times \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} \times [0,1],

with E_{\text{own}} and E_{\text{sm}} measured by the VEQ ownership and body-schema-change subscales, and E_{\text{loc}} by a self-location item.

Proposition 2″ (Three-factor structure). The three-factor model is preferred to a one-factor model if \Delta \text{BIC} > 10 across at least two independent samples.

Refutation condition R2″. The three-factor model is refuted if the one-factor model is retained across both samples.

3.2 Supernumerary limb as the dissociation test

Prism adaptation is replaced by an independent supernumerary robotic finger. An independent extra limb can be embodied, but ownership is more difficult to induce than for a substitute limb, and the body image accepts the extra limb while the body schema does not. The dissociation test is:

· Passive congruent visuo-tactile stimulation of the supernumerary finger \to raises E_{\text{own}}, leaves E_{\text{sm}} unchanged.
· Active motor training with the supernumerary finger \to raises E_{\text{sm}} (generalization to novel configurations), may or may not raise E_{\text{own}}.

This is a cleaner test of Axiom 1 than prism adaptation because it manipulates ownership and sensorimotor integration within the same artificial effector.

