Appendix A — Sensorimotor Adaptation: Corrected Derivations and the Consistency–Sensitivity Mediation Model

A.1 State-space model and fixed point

Let x_t \in \mathbb{R} be the internal estimate of the perturbation, r_t \in \mathbb{R} the true perturbation, e_t = r_t - x_t + \varepsilon_t the sensory prediction error, a \in (0,1) the retention factor, and b_t > 0 the error sensitivity. The learning rule is

x_{t+1} = a x_t + b_t e_t.

For constant r_t = r, b_t = b, and \varepsilon_t = 0,

x_{t+1} = a x_t + b(r - x_t) = (a-b)x_t + b r.

Fixed point. Setting x_{t+1} = x_t = x^\star,

x^\star = (a-b)x^\star + b r
\;\Longrightarrow\;
(1 - a + b)x^\star = b r
\;\Longrightarrow\;
x^\star = \frac{b}{1 - a + b} r.

The residual error is

e^\star = r - x^\star
= r - \frac{b}{1 - a + b} r
= \frac{1 - a}{1 - a + b} r.

The v1.0 formulas x^\star = \frac{b}{1-a}r and e^\star = \frac{1-a-b}{1-a}r omitted the +b term in the denominator. For a=0.9, b=0.2, the v1.0 fixed point gives x^\star = 2r and a negative residual, whereas the correct fixed point is x^\star = \tfrac{2}{3}r and e^\star = \tfrac{1}{3}r.

A.2 Stability

The homogeneous dynamics are x_{t+1} = (a-b)x_t. The fixed point is stable if and only if

|a - b| < 1.

Since a \in (0,1) and b>0, this is satisfied for b < 1+a. For the empirically estimated values a \approx 0.91 and b \approx 0.037, a-b \approx 0.87, well within the stable region.

A.3 Stationary variance

With stochastic r_t and independent motor noise \varepsilon_t,

x_{t+1} = (a-b)x_t + b r_t + b \varepsilon_t.

Let \sigma_r^2 = \operatorname{Var}(r_t) and \sigma_\varepsilon^2 = \operatorname{Var}(\varepsilon_t). If r_t and \varepsilon_t are white, the stationary variance is

\operatorname{Var}(x_\infty) = \frac{b^2(\sigma_r^2 + \sigma_\varepsilon^2)}{1 - (a-b)^2}.

The v1.0 formula b^2 \sigma_r^2/(1-a^2) used the wrong AR coefficient a instead of (a-b) and omitted the motor-noise contribution \sigma_\varepsilon^2. The corrected variance is substantially smaller: with a=0.9, b=0.2, \sigma_r^2=1, and \sigma_\varepsilon^2=0.1, the v1.0 formula gives 0.21, whereas the correct formula gives 0.04/0.03 \approx 1.33 for the noise-free case and 0.044/(1-0.49) \approx 0.086 for the noise case — a qualitative reversal in scale, not a minor correction.

A.4 Consistency functional for Gaussian errors

Definition A.1. The consistency functional is

\kappa(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{e \sim P_\theta}\left[ \exp\left( -\frac{\|e - \bar{e}\|^2}{2\sigma_c^2} \right) \right],

where \bar{e} is the modal error vector and \sigma_c^2 is a pre-registered consistency bandwidth.

For a Gaussian error distribution e \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \Sigma),

\kappa(\theta)
= \left| I + \frac{\Sigma}{\sigma_c^2} \right|^{-1/2}
\exp\left( -\frac{1}{2}(\mu - \bar{e})^\top \left(\Sigma + \sigma_c^2 I\right)^{-1}(\mu - \bar{e}) \right).

If \mu = \bar{e} (the modal error is the mean), this reduces to

\kappa(\theta) = \prod_{i=1}^{m} \left(1 + \frac{\lambda_i(\Sigma)}{\sigma_c^2}\right)^{-1/2},

where \lambda_i(\Sigma) are the eigenvalues of the error covariance. Consistency is therefore a monotone decreasing function of the error covariance eigenvalues: higher variance produces lower \kappa, as required.

A.5 Trial-to-trial error sensitivity update

The memory-of-errors model of Albert et al. partitions the error space into bins and updates sensitivity locally. A continuous approximation is

b_t = b_0 + \sum_{k=1}^{K} c_k \, \Delta b_{t,k},
\qquad
\Delta b_{t,k} = \alpha \, \phi_k(e_t) \, \Delta b_{t-1,k} - \beta \, \Delta b_{t-1,k},

where \phi_k(e) = \exp(-\|e - \bar{e}_k\|^2/(2\sigma_k^2)) is a radial basis function centred on bin k, \alpha is the upregulation rate, and \beta is the decay rate. The local nature of the update — sensitivity changes only for errors near the experienced error — is essential; it is what distinguishes memory-of-errors from a global learning-rate modulation.

Proposition A.1. Under the local update, the steady-state sensitivity is

b_\infty(e) = b_0 + \frac{\alpha}{\beta} \sum_k \phi_k(e) \, \mathbb{E}[\phi_k(e_t)],

which is a smoothed version of the error distribution. High-variance environments spread probability mass across bins, reducing \mathbb{E}[\phi_k(e_t)] for every k and therefore reducing b_\infty(e). This is the formal mechanism behind the claim that “total implicit adaptation decreased in the high-variance environment due to changes in error sensitivity, not in forgetting”.

A.6 The NLMC rival and the matched-error-size condition

The non-linear motor-correction (NLMC) model posits a static non-linear correction function f(e) such that the trial-to-trial change in reach angle is f(e_t), with error sensitivity b(e) = f(e)/e decreasing monotonically in error size. When perturbation variance increases, the error distribution shifts into the low-sensitivity region of f, and the average correction is attenuated without any history-dependent change in b.

Formal distinction. Under memory-of-errors, b_t depends on the sequence of errors: a consistent sequence of the same signed error increases b_t, while an inconsistent sequence decreases it. Under NLMC, b_t = f(e_t)/e_t depends only on the current error, not on its history. The two models make opposite predictions for a design in which the error-size distribution is held fixed but the temporal consistency is manipulated:

· Memory-of-errors: higher consistency \kappa → higher b_t → lower asymptotic residual.
· NLMC: with the error-size distribution fixed, b_t is fixed on average → no effect of consistency.

Matched-error-size control. The factorial latency design must therefore include an arm in which the marginal error-size distribution is matched across consistency conditions, either by construction (error-clamp trials with identical error magnitudes but different temporal ordering) or by post-hoc stratification. This is the operational implementation of the NLMC control.

A.7 Mediation decomposition

The mediation model is

b_t = b_0 + \beta_1 \kappa(\theta_t) + \boldsymbol{\beta}_2^\top \mathbf{z}_t + \eta_t,
\qquad
e^\star = \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \kappa(\theta) + \gamma_2 \bar{b} + \boldsymbol{\gamma}_3^\top \mathbf{z} + \zeta,

where \mathbf{z} includes mean error magnitude and the NLMC-predicted correction \bar{f}(e) = \mathbb{E}[f(e)].

Proposition A.2 (Mediation). The total effect of consistency on the asymptote decomposes as

\frac{\partial e^\star}{\partial \kappa}
= \underbrace{\gamma_1}_{\text{direct}}
+ \underbrace{\beta_1 \gamma_2}_{\text{indirect via } b_t}.

The memory-of-errors hypothesis requires \beta_1 \gamma_2 > 0 and significant. The NLMC alternative requires \gamma_1 = 0 after conditioning on \mathbf{z}.

Refutation condition R1″ (formal). Proposition 1″ is refuted if (i) the bias-corrected bootstrap confidence interval for \gamma_1 contains zero after residualizing on mean error magnitude and \bar{f}(e), or (ii) the indirect effect \beta_1 \gamma_2 is not positive and significant at the pre-registered level. The first condition tests whether consistency has any effect beyond error-size sampling; the second tests whether the effect operates through b_t rather than through a direct route.

A.8 Why the prediction-gain alternative was rejected as the primary variable

The v1.0 quantity

\mathcal{M}(e) = \frac{\mathcal{I}(e_{t-\tau:t-1}; e_t)}{\mathcal{H}(e_t)}

is not bounded in [0,1] because differential entropy \mathcal{H}(e_t) can be negative for narrow distributions. For a Gaussian AR(1) with autocorrelation \rho,

\mathcal{M}(e) = \frac{-\tfrac{1}{2}\ln(1-\rho^2)}{\tfrac{1}{2}\ln(2\pi e \sigma^2)},

which depends on the noise scale \sigma^2 even when \rho is fixed. The prediction gain

G(e) = 1 - \frac{\operatorname{Var}(e_t \mid e_{t-\tau:t-1})}{\operatorname{Var}(e_t)} = \rho^2

is bounded and scale-free, and it is retained as a control variable, not as the primary predictor. The primary predictor is \kappa(\theta), because it captures the history-dependent modulation of sensitivity that is the empirically supported mechanism.

