Appendix B — Agency Measurement Model: Identification, Fit, and Decision Rule

B.1 The one-factor model

Let \mathbf{A} = (A_{\text{SA}}, A_{\text{IB}}, A_{\text{exp}})^\top be the vector of standardized agency indicators. The one-factor model is

\mathbf{A} = \boldsymbol{\lambda} A + \boldsymbol{\varepsilon},
\qquad
\boldsymbol{\varepsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \boldsymbol{\Theta}),
\qquad
\boldsymbol{\Theta} = \operatorname{diag}(\theta_1, \theta_2, \theta_3).

Identification. The model has p(p+1)/2 = 6 unique covariance elements and p + p = 6 parameters (3 loadings + 3 residual variances), so it is just-identified unless a loading is fixed. We fix \lambda_1 = 1, yielding df = 3 and a testable model.

The implied covariance is

\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \boldsymbol{\lambda}\phi\boldsymbol{\lambda}^\top + \boldsymbol{\Theta},

where \phi = \operatorname{Var}(A).

B.2 Fit function and test statistic

Let \mathbf{S} be the sample covariance. The maximum-likelihood fit function is

F_{\text{ML}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})
= \ln|\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(\boldsymbol{\theta})|
+ \operatorname{tr}\left(\mathbf{S}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}(\boldsymbol{\theta})^{-1}\right)
- \ln|\mathbf{S}| - p.

The likelihood-ratio test statistic is

T = (N-1) F_{\text{ML}}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\theta}}) \;\overset{H_0}{\sim}\; \chi^2(df),

with df = 3.

B.3 RMSEA and CFI

\text{RMSEA} = \sqrt{\max\left(0, \frac{T - df}{df(N-1)}\right)},
\qquad
\text{CFI} = 1 - \frac{\max(0, T - df)}{\max(0, T_0 - df_0)},

where T_0 and df_0 are the test statistic and degrees of freedom of the independence model.

B.4 Decision rule and consequences

Decision rule. The one-factor model is not rejected if RMSEA < .08 and CFI > .95. If either threshold is exceeded, the one-factor model is rejected, and the three indicators are analyzed as separate endpoints. The divergence pattern — which indicator dissociates from which — is reported as a finding.

Proposition B.1 (Expected rejection). Given the documented dissociation between intentional binding and explicit agency judgments, and between sensory attenuation and explicit judgments, the one-factor model is expected to be rejected. The confirmatory value of the measurement model is therefore not in confirming a latent agency factor but in formally establishing the divergence that the v1.0 framework assumed away.

Consequence for shared-control studies. If the one-factor model is rejected, the agency–authority curve (Proposition 4″) is estimated separately for each indicator. The prediction of an interior maximum is tested on A_{\text{exp}} and, where available, on a composite of A_{\text{exp}} and A_{\text{SA}}; A_{\text{IB}} is analyzed as a predictability index, not an agency index, because intentional binding in human–computer dyads tracks sensorimotor predictability rather than authorship.

