Appendix C — Body-Model Profile: Multi-Factor Structure and the Supernumerary Dissociation

C.1 Three-factor VEQ model

Let \mathbf{y} = (y_1, \dots, y_{12})^\top be the twelve VEQ items. The three-factor model is

\mathbf{y} = \boldsymbol{\Lambda}\boldsymbol{\eta} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon},
\qquad
\boldsymbol{\eta} = (\eta_{\text{own}}, \eta_{\text{sm}}, \eta_{\text{loc}})^\top,

with \boldsymbol{\Lambda} a 12 \times 3 loading matrix and \boldsymbol{\epsilon} residual noise. The VEQ has a confirmed three-factor structure — ownership, agency, and perceived body-schema change — in a CFA with N=196 across three experiments. Self-location is added as a separate item because it is dissociable from ownership and is not captured by the VEQ.

C.2 BIC comparison

For a model with log-likelihood \ln L and k free parameters,

\text{BIC} = -2\ln L + k\ln N.

Proposition C.1. The three-factor model is preferred to the one-factor model if

\Delta\text{BIC} = \text{BIC}_{1} - \text{BIC}_{3} > 10

across at least two independent samples. The threshold of 10 corresponds to “strong” evidence on the standard Kass–Raftery scale. If the one-factor model is retained in both samples, Proposition 2″ is refuted.

C.3 Supernumerary dissociation design

Design. A 2 \times 2 mixed design: between-subjects factor training type (passive congruent visuo-tactile stimulation vs. active motor training with the supernumerary finger), within-subject factor time (pre vs. post). Dependent variables: E_{\text{own}}, E_{\text{sm}}, and E_{\text{loc}}, measured separately.

Proposition C.2 (Dissociation). If E_{\text{own}} and E_{\text{sm}} are dissociable dimensions of the body-model profile, then:

1. Passive stimulation produces a larger increase in E_{\text{own}} than in E_{\text{sm}}.
2. Active training produces a larger increase in E_{\text{sm}} than in E_{\text{own}}.

Formally, the training type × dimension interaction is significant:

\big(E_{\text{own}}^{\text{post}} - E_{\text{own}}^{\text{pre}}\big)_{\text{passive}}
- \big(E_{\text{own}}^{\text{post}} - E_{\text{own}}^{\text{pre}}\big)_{\text{active}}
>
\big(E_{\text{sm}}^{\text{post}} - E_{\text{sm}}^{\text{pre}}\big)_{\text{passive}}
- \big(E_{\text{sm}}^{\text{post}} - E_{\text{sm}}^{\text{pre}}\big)_{\text{active}}.

Refutation condition R2″ (dissociation form). The dissociation is refuted if the interaction is null or in the opposite direction, i.e., if passive stimulation raises E_{\text{sm}} more than E_{\text{own}}, or active training raises E_{\text{own}} more than E_{\text{sm}}.

Why the supernumerary limb replaces prism adaptation. Prism adaptation changes the sensorimotor mapping for the biological arm; it does not manipulate ownership of an artificial effector. The supernumerary finger allows ownership and sensorimotor integration to be manipulated within the same artificial effector, making the dissociation test internally valid.

