Appendix D — Shared Control: Q-CBF Minimal-Intervention Authority and the Agency–Authority Curve

D.1 The authority half-line

The blended command is

u(\alpha) = \alpha u_r + (1-\alpha)u_h,
\qquad \alpha \in [0,1].

Let the Q-CBF constraint be Q(s, u(\alpha)) \geq 0. For a Q-CBF that is affine in u,

Q(s,u) = q_0(s) + q_1(s)^\top u,

we have

Q(s,u(\alpha))
= q_0 + q_1^\top u_h + \alpha \, q_1^\top (u_r - u_h).

Proposition D.1 (Feasible set is an interval). The feasible authority set is

\mathcal{A}(s) = \{\alpha \in [0,1] : Q(s,u(\alpha)) \geq 0\},

which is a closed interval (possibly empty). It is not, in general, a singleton or the whole of [0,1]. The v1.0 claim that the problem is infeasible if and only if the robot policy cannot satisfy the CBF is false: infeasibility occurs when no convex combination of u_h and u_r satisfies the constraint, which can happen even when u_r alone violates it and u_h alone violates it.

Feasibility condition. \mathcal{A}(s) \neq \emptyset if and only if

\max_{\alpha \in [0,1]} Q(s,u(\alpha)) \geq 0.

Since Q is affine in \alpha, the maximum is attained at an endpoint:

\max\{Q(s,u_h),\, Q(s,u_r)\} \geq 0.

Thus feasibility requires either the human command or the robot command to be safe, or a convex combination of them to be safe.

D.2 Minimal-intervention projection

The authority solves

\alpha^\star = \arg\min_{\alpha \in \mathcal{A}(s)} \; \|\alpha u_r + (1-\alpha)u_h - u_h\|^2
= \arg\min_{\alpha \in \mathcal{A}(s)} \; \alpha^2 \|u_r - u_h\|^2.

If u_r \neq u_h, this is equivalent to minimizing \alpha^2 over the feasible interval. Let

\alpha_{\min} = \inf \mathcal{A}(s),
\qquad
\alpha_{\max} = \sup \mathcal{A}(s).

The projection is

\alpha^\star =
\begin{cases}
0, & 0 \in \mathcal{A}(s), \\
\alpha_{\min}, & 0 \notin \mathcal{A}(s) \text{ and } \alpha_{\min} \leq 1, \\
\text{infeasible}, & \mathcal{A}(s) = \emptyset.
\end{cases}

Proposition D.2 (Minimal intervention). When the human command is safe (Q(s,u_h) \geq 0), \alpha^\star = 0: the robot does not intervene. When the human command is unsafe but the robot command is safe, \alpha^\star = \alpha_{\min}, the smallest authority that restores safety. This is the formal meaning of “minimal intervention”: the filter deviates from the human command only to the extent required by the Q-CBF constraint.

D.3 The interior agency maximum

Proposition D.3 (Concave agency–authority curve). Under the alignment account, agency is a concave function of \alpha with an interior maximum. A parsimonious formalization is

A(\alpha) = A_0 + \beta \, \delta(\alpha) - \gamma \, \alpha^2,

where \delta(\alpha) \in [0,1] is the intent alignment between the blended command and the human’s inferred goal, \beta > 0 captures the agency benefit of aligned assistance, and \gamma > 0 captures the agency cost of override.

If alignment is approximately linear in \alpha over the relevant range, \delta(\alpha) \approx \delta_0 + \delta_1 \alpha, then

A(\alpha) \approx A_0 + \beta \delta_0 + \beta \delta_1 \alpha - \gamma \alpha^2,

which is concave with interior maximum

\alpha^\star_A = \frac{\beta \delta_1}{2\gamma},

provided 0 < \beta \delta_1/(2\gamma) < 1.

Refutation condition R4″ (formal). The interior maximum is refuted if the estimated second derivative of A(\alpha) is non-negative (convex or linear), or if the maximum is attained at \alpha=0 or \alpha=1 in a pre-registered study with agency as a co-primary endpoint and a pre-specified intent-alignment metric.

D.4 Why agency is a co-primary endpoint, not a constraint

If agency were entered as a constraint A(\alpha) \geq A_{\min}, the optimization would be

\min_{\alpha} \; \alpha^2 \|u_r - u_h\|^2
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
Q(s,u(\alpha)) \geq 0,\;
A(\alpha) \geq A_{\min}.

This would treat agency as a feasibility condition, which is not justified: low agency is not a safety violation. Agency is better treated as an outcome to be measured and modeled, with its own prediction (Proposition D.3), rather than as a constraint that can render the safety problem infeasible. This is why v4.0 makes agency a co-primary endpoint of the shared-control study, not a constraint in the authority optimization.

