Appendix E — Delay-Compensated Safety: Prediction Error Bounds and Robustness

E.1 Delayed system and predictor

Consider the input-delayed system

\dot{x}(t) = f(x(t)) + g(x(t)) \, u(t-\tau),

where \tau > 0 is the communication or actuation delay. The delay-compensated architecture combines a delay predictor, a disturbance observer, and a CBF-QP. Let \hat{x}(t+\tau \mid t) be the predicted state at t+\tau given information up to t. The CBF condition is imposed on the predicted state:

\dot{h}(\hat{x}(t+\tau \mid t)) + \gamma h(\hat{x}(t+\tau \mid t)) \geq 0.

E.2 Lipschitz robustness of the CBF condition

Assumption E.1. The prediction error is bounded:

\|\hat{x}(t+\tau \mid t) - x(t+\tau)\| \leq \delta
\qquad \forall t.

Assumption E.2. The barrier function h is Lipschitz with constant L_h:

|h(x) - h(y)| \leq L_h \|x - y\|.

Proposition E.1 (Robust CBF margin). Under Assumptions E.1–E.2, if the predicted state satisfies the strengthened condition

\dot{h}(\hat{x}) + \gamma h(\hat{x}) \geq L_h \delta + \gamma L_h \delta,

then the true state satisfies

\dot{h}(x) + \gamma h(x) \geq 0.

Proof. By Lipschitz continuity,

h(x) \geq h(\hat{x}) - L_h \delta,
\qquad
\dot{h}(x) \geq \dot{h}(\hat{x}) - L_h \delta.

Therefore

\dot{h}(x) + \gamma h(x)
\geq \dot{h}(\hat{x}) + \gamma h(\hat{x}) - L_h \delta - \gamma L_h \delta
\geq 0,

where the last inequality uses the strengthened condition. ∎

E.3 ISS-style margin

The strengthened condition can be written as

\dot{h}(\hat{x}) + \gamma h(\hat{x}) \geq (1+\gamma)L_h \delta.

The term (1+\gamma)L_h \delta is the robustness margin. It quantifies how much the predicted CBF condition must be tightened to guarantee the true condition under a prediction error of size \delta. The disturbance observer reduces \delta; the delay predictor reduces the systematic component of \delta; the margin accounts for the residual.

Proposition E.2 (Validity condition). The delay-compensated CBF remains valid if

\delta \leq \frac{\dot{h}(\hat{x}) + \gamma h(\hat{x})}{(1+\gamma)L_h}.

If this condition is violated, the safety filter cannot certify safety and must enter a safe-stop mode. This is the formal counterpart of the requirement that “the robustness margin against prediction error is pre-specified.”

