Appendix F — Human Transfer: COIN Metrics

F.1 Why PAC-Bayes does not apply to human subjects

PAC-Bayes bounds are statements about the generalization of a learner that selects a hypothesis from a hypothesis class. The bound has the form

R(Q) \leq \hat{R}(Q) + \sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{KL}(Q\|P) + \ln(2n/\delta)}{2n}},

where Q is a posterior over hypotheses and \hat{R} is the empirical risk. Human sensorimotor adaptation is not well described as posterior inference over a hypothesis class; it is better described as contextual inference over latent contexts, with memory creation, updating, and expression controlled by a single computation. Applying PAC-Bayes to human transfer is therefore a category error.

F.2 COIN-based transfer metrics

Savings. Let T_{\text{naive}} be the number of trials to reach a criterion in embodiment B without prior A experience, and T_{\text{transfer}} the number of trials after prior A experience. The savings index is

S = \frac{T_{\text{naive}} - T_{\text{transfer}}}{T_{\text{naive}}}.

Positive S indicates faster re-learning; negative S indicates interference.

Spontaneous recovery. After washout of A and a retention interval \Delta, the re-emergence of A-adapted behaviour is measured as the error at the first post-retention trial relative to the washout asymptote:

\text{SR}(\Delta) = \frac{e_{\text{post-retention}} - e_{\text{washout}}}{e_{\text{adaptation}} - e_{\text{washout}}}.

COIN predicts spontaneous recovery because the context associated with A is partially reinstated after the retention interval.

Normalized transfer gap. The v1.0 metric

\Delta T = R_{\mathcal{T}}(\pi_{\mathcal{T}}^{\text{after } \mathcal{S}}) - R_{\mathcal{T}}(\pi_{\mathcal{T}}^{\text{baseline}})

is retained as a tertiary metric, normalized by the baseline learning rate.

F.3 What COIN predicts that PAC-Bayes does not

COIN predicts anterograde interference (learning A slows subsequent learning of B when A and B share contexts) and context-dependent single-trial learning. These are not captured by a PAC-Bayes bound, which is agnostic about the temporal structure of the learning problem. The transfer experiment should therefore include an anterograde interference probe: after learning A, learn B and measure whether the B learning rate is reduced relative to a B-only baseline. COIN predicts a reduction; a PAC-Bayes account makes no prediction.

