Appendix G — Robot Policy Transfer: Transition-Kernel PAC-Bayes Bound

G.1 Discrepancy in transition-kernel space

Definition G.1. The embodiment discrepancy is

\operatorname{dis}_{\text{emb}}(\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{T})
= \sup_{x,u} \left\| P_{\mathcal{S}}(\cdot \mid x,u) - P_{\mathcal{T}}(\cdot \mid x,u) \right\|_{\text{TV}},

where P_{\mathcal{S}} and P_{\mathcal{T}} are the action-conditioned transition kernels of the source and target embodiments.

This replaces the marginal input discrepancy \operatorname{dis}_\rho(\mathcal{S}_{\mathbf{X}}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathbf{X}}) of v1.0. Two embodiments can have identical state marginals but different action-conditioned dynamics — e.g., the same kinematic workspace with different inertia or latency — and the input discrepancy would be zero while the transfer gap is large.

G.2 Bound and proof sketch

Theorem G.1 (Transition-kernel PAC-Bayes bound). For any prior P over policies and any posterior Q, with probability at least 1-\delta over the draw of the source sample of size n,

R_{\mathcal{T}}(Q)
\leq R_{\mathcal{S}}(Q)
+ \operatorname{dis}_{\text{emb}}(\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{T})
+ \lambda_{\text{emb}}
+ \sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{KL}(Q\|P) + \ln(2n/\delta)}{2n}},

where \lambda_{\text{emb}} is the best joint error achievable by a policy in the hypothesis class under the transition-kernel constraint.

Proof sketch. Decompose the target risk:

R_{\mathcal{T}}(Q)
= R_{\mathcal{S}}(Q)
+ \big(R_{\mathcal{T}}(Q) - R_{\mathcal{S}}(Q)\big).

The difference is bounded by the total-variation discrepancy between the transition kernels, because the expected loss under Q is a bounded functional of the transition kernel:

\big|R_{\mathcal{T}}(Q) - R_{\mathcal{S}}(Q)\big|
\leq \operatorname{dis}_{\text{emb}}(\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{T})
+ \lambda_{\text{emb}},

where \lambda_{\text{emb}} absorbs the approximation error of the hypothesis class. The source risk R_{\mathcal{S}}(Q) is bounded by the standard PAC-Bayes bound applied to the source sample:

R_{\mathcal{S}}(Q)
\leq \hat{R}_{\mathcal{S}}(Q)
+ \sqrt{\frac{\operatorname{KL}(Q\|P) + \ln(2n/\delta)}{2n}}.

Combining the two inequalities yields the theorem. ∎

G.3 Meta-learning decomposition

When the prior P is learned from M source embodiments, the KL term decomposes into an environment-level term and a task-level term:

\operatorname{KL}(Q\|P)
\approx \frac{C_{\text{env}}}{M}
+ \frac{C_{\text{task}}}{n},

where C_{\text{env}} captures the complexity of the embodiment distribution and C_{\text{task}} captures the complexity of the task within an embodiment. This is the correct form of the “diversity improves transfer” argument: diversity reduces the environment-level term, but it does not eliminate the task-level term. The v1.0 claim that diversity improves zero-shot transfer is therefore a hypothesis about the relative magnitudes of these two terms, not a theorem.

G.4 Why the bound is diagnostic, not predictive

The bound contains \lambda_{\text{emb}}, which is the best joint error achievable by the hypothesis class across both embodiments. This quantity is not estimable from finite data without strong assumptions on the hypothesis class. The bound therefore cannot be used to predict the sign or magnitude of the transfer gap. It can be used diagnostically: if the bound is large, at least one of the three terms is large, and the experimenter can measure which one. This is why v4.0 confines the PAC-Bayes bound to L5 and treats the empirical transfer gap as the primary quantity.

